  "summary": "Traditional Persian ventilation towers using wind to cool interior spaces through passive design.",
  "description": "Characterized by distinctive tall towers with multiple wind-catching openings oriented to catch prevailing breezes, channeling cool air into buildings while expelling hot air. Developed in desert regions to provide natural cooling without mechanical systems, representing sophisticated adaptation to extreme climate conditions.",
  "culturalContext": "The badgir (windcatcher) towers of Yazd, Iran — a UNESCO-listed desert architecture — ventilate buildings by capturing airflow at height and directing it down shafts over water or through earth-cooled basements, passively air-conditioning for a millennium. Yazd's skyline of crenellated wind towers over adobe roofs became Iran's emblem of indigenous engineering; paired with qanat underground channels and ab anbar cistern domes, the system is studied today as low-carbon precedent. Decorated with badgir \"crown\" lattices, the towers marry physics and skyline poetry.",
